\f1\fs22 By dusk the air was thick with the smell of trodden grass, spilled beer, fried chicken and grilled burger patties. A nine-piece with two drummers had come off stage and gone straight into a second set in the corner, better than their onstage performance. The drummers played on mats and esky lids while the horns blew softer. Out past the canvas, up on the VIP platform Chanta knew his throne sat empty. He could at least give Sarah that much. That he might be hiding from her never occurred to him and his writer\'92s block could get taxed.\
Chanta was startled by the High Elvis sitting on an esky, as if he\'92d been there all day. He must have snuck up under cover of the Elvisette sitting on Chanta\'92s lap.\
\'93They fought all afternoon, out there,\'94 the High Elvis said, mild as a cricket commentator, then took a long, slow draught of Pepsi, and waited for Chanta to look at him squarely. \'93There would have been less fighting if there were more lyrics.\'94 He turned his hand over, examined the nails. \'93We\'92re here to unite the Elvi for a cause, not wallow in our old rabbling ways. And yet we keep showing the world we\'92re worthy of being labelled an underclass. One song, Great Teacher. About the Dawkins, about the whole beautiful accident of us, about evolution showing us that everyone here is no better or worse than anyone else. We\'92re all \'85 just here.\'94 He said it the way a doctor names a dose, without fervour or bombast. \'93The night wants it. That\'92s all.\'94\
Chanta believed the High Elvis believed his own words.\
Words. Chanta knew few of them compared to Sarah or Kathy or any Elvi, but he knew how even the few he had could be twisted to mean almost anything. The Holy Rollers were taking words of hope and forgiveness and making weapons of them\'97weapons of damnation and passicution (if that was the right word; he was only partly sure that people got passicuted).\
An Elvisette gave him a fresh drink; he twisted the umbrella in his drink, and thought of twisted words. He wanted to say yes, and he wanted the yes to be true. But even Chanta could see the Evolution Elvi were making a religion of fighting a religion (though seeing still made his head hurt in ways no white powder could numb). They wanted to make a profit of the Original Dawkins and were worshipping Little Clinton in ways no kid his age\'97or even older\'97should be worshipped.\
He blamed the Sages, and even Solo\'92s promise he would be reconciled with Sarah seemed farther away the closer she got. Even if he could find the courage to go and claim his throne next to Sarah and the golden aura of Little Clinton, he doubted his writer\'92s block would be cleared; all this religious fighting among the Elvi drained him of any joy for writing lyrics. He was caught. 
\f2\i Caught in a trap between two churches
\f1\i0 , he thought. And they wouldn\'92t let him walk away.\
Before he knew it, Chanta sang, \'93Because you\'92re praying too much...\'94 The line didn\'92t feel finished, so he added, \'93\'85baby.\'94\
The High Elvis leaned in. \'93Did you just sing something?\'94\
Chanta returned to his body and found his legs going to sleep. He stretched and adjusted the Elvisette in his lap. \'93What? No.\'94\
\'93Yes, you were,\'94 the High Elvis said, still sounding like he was reading a cricket scorecard.\
\'93I was just muttering,\'94 Chanta said.\
\'93Well, keep muttering then. Mutter me one song in time for my closing set and I won\'92t have you banned from the Ghetto.\'94 It was a threat that mortified Chanta, delivered in a tone of someone summarising, 
\f2\i Lillee caught Willey bowled Dilley
\f1\i0 , 
\f2\i nineteen
\f1\i0 .\
