\f1\fs22 If he needed any more evidence he couldn\'92t write, that stupid little ditty he tapped on his device while he fled around the arena trying not to think about the High Elvis\'92s ultimatum proved it once and for all\'97not even after polishing a mirrored platter, which inspired the last line.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 There was only one thing for it: he\'92d have to go up on the VIP platform and let Sarah have at him for being too rich for his own good. Once he\'92d made the decision, his head cleared. Avoiding Sarah was stupid and cowardly and, anyway, he knew she\'92d get over his new-found wealth eventually.\
\'93Great Teacher!\'94
\f2\i \

\f1\i0 \'93Great Teacher!\'94
\f2\i \

\f1\i0 He was hailed only twice as he worked his way through the crowd that mostly had no idea who he was, and how he\'92d basically made all this possible.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 No thanks or adulation sought or required, mind you, just ignore me and go about playing your air guitars, which I also thought of selling, by the way, so I\'92m looking forward to a nice little licensing arrangement there, if you must know.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\i0 \cf2 There was no reason money should come between him and Sarah, especially since he\'92d ordered that portrait of Jamison Hardy burned, although he\'92d learned later that Fisher had quietly donated it to the art gallery only an axe throw over the tree line from here\'97perhaps he could go burn the art gallery down when the concert was over, that\'92d fix everything.\
Burning down an art gallery was at least a thing a man could do. Asking Sarah about the portrait was not. He\'92d been there when she first saw it, Sarah, who returned serial killers to their maker the way other women returned faulty toasters, gripping his doorframe like the floor had developed personal plans for her. You have no idea what that man did to me. A man a hundred years dead. There was a question in all of that somewhere, and Chanta knew what he did with questions like it: he kept a drawer for them, the same one he kept shut for police. He shut it. If Sarah ever wanted that drawer opened, she knew where he lived, which put her one up on his songwriting talent.\
He kept muttering as he climbed the stairs, and he kept climbing while he muttered. Until he reached the top.\
\'93Great Teacher!\'94 said someone with too much beard, too little baby inside, and who was, but shouldn\'92t be, wearing a pair of headphones around their neck. \'93You do us a great honour!\'94\
It took a few more moments for his rant-fog to clear. He looked over the edge and saw the VIP platform below. Apparently, he was so determined to confront Sarah that even greater determination was required to hijack the whole intention and make him ascend the wrong stairs.\
All that determination deflated him; all he could do was peer over at what were undoubtedly the heads of Sarah, Kathy, some soft dude Kathy was snuggling and\'97
\f2\i taxing tax me, is that Eloise Shakti
\f1\i0 ? There was also some scrawny Elvis dude sitting on his throne.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

