\f1\i0 \cf2 \'93Great Teacher?\'94 The sound engineer asked.\
\'93Um, carry on,\'94 Chanta said eventually. \'93Do you mind if I just hang around a while?\'94\
\'93It\'92d be our honour, Great Teacher.\'94 The sound engineer looked at Chanta a little harder. \'93Ah, it looks like you got a little something on your face,\'94 he said while pointing at his own. \'93Have you been licking a plate of coke, or something?\'94\
Chanta blinked. \'93Hey, this is a party\'85 let\'92s not be making accusations about what\'92s licked who now.\'94\
After the crew went back to all hands on desks, Chanta peered over the VIP platform again. Down there was his wellspring. His muse. Little Clinton, who gave him the songs, who had always given him the songs, seven months along and one floor down on an adjoining platform. Chanta couldn\'92t see Little Clinton\'92s golden orange aura, obscured by the back of Sarah\'92s throne, probably.\
No inspiration came to Chanta, only memories of the desecrated Memphis Mafia Bar, where Lonnie used to pull his beer before he\'92d said hello, where a man could be nobody in peace. He thought of Pastor Rex Humble piously clipping sideburns and saving the hair in a bowl like relics. The Holy Rollers had taken his favourite haunt, hollowed it out and made it a church.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 Because you\'92re praying too much\'85baby.
\f1\i0 \
That was the second time that day Chanta had sung those lines.\
Anger came up in him, hot and clean.
\f2\i  
\f1\i0 The Evolution Elvi and the Holy Rollers were two heads of the one coin.\
Chanta thought of the Oath of the Warranty, which he\'92d heard so many times, he knew it \'93verbitem\'94:\

\f2\i I shall always strive for the perfection of the Music\
As long as it is not gospel\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\i0 \cf2 But Chanta knew it wasn\'92t just gospel music that the original Grayling Corp warranty excluded. He pulled out his device and went searching; he found the warranty in no time.\
\'93
\f2\i This Warranty is immediately null and void, and the ELVIS shall be immediately reclaimable
\f1\i0 ,\'94 Chanta read quietly aloud, as he always did when he needed to actually understand what he read. \'93Yeah, yeah, yeah.\'94 Impatiently, he scrolled and scanned. \'93
\f2\i Exposing the ELVIS to amphetamine, crystal methamphetamine, cocaine or stimulant patch.
\f1\i0 \'94 Chanta laughed. \'93Good luck with that, Grayling, you dead taxwit.\'94 He read on. \'93
\f2\i Exposing the ELVIS to religion or religious memes or icons including but not limited to religious texts, psalms, hymns, dashboard figurines, tacky paintings of a blue-eyed Jesus, or gospel music of any kind. 
\f1\i0 Ha, I knew it!
\f2\i \'94  
\f1\i0 \
The warranty didn\'92t just ban gospel music; it banned any kind of religious exposure. Chanta\'92s blood chilled. Had he opened the door to religion when he researched the Original Dawkins that day back in the recording studio?\
As far as he recalled, Dawkins was no fan of religion of any kind. The Elvi should be fighting Latter Day Capitalism, not each other. Yet turning evolution into a religion took the Elvi about forty-six minutes.\
He would not, afterwards, call it dramatic. One moment Chanta was slumped against the railing with a biro he did not remember picking up, and the next he had simply gone up out of himself, cheerful and weightless. He looked back once and saw himself down there, folded over the desk, scribbling, and then he was flying, out and down over the lawn, and it was marvellous, and he thought, with a clear practical priority, that he could murder a cheeseburger. Then he was on the VIP platform. On his throne crowned with melted bobbleheads. The scrawny Elvis dude was nowhere to be seen but, since Chanta was pretty sure he was hallucinating, where he sat didn\'92t matter a tax. He sat beside Sarah; she didn\'92t turn, she didn\'92t see him.\
The words came, too fast to catch, a flood with no bottom. Up in the tower, Real-body-Chanta plucked a festival-branded coffee-ringed notepad off the sound desk, and scribbled automatic words, the biro gouging into the paper. He wrote fast, faster than out-of-body-Chanta could recall real-body-Chanta ever writing.\
Out-of-body-Chanta rejoined the real one barrelling down the tower stairs with the notepad crushed to his chest, and nearly threw them both off with the impact. He ran and pushed his way through the crowd, away from his throne, away from Sarah (which was a relief) and Little Clinton (which wasn\'92t), back towards the stage and the hospitality tent and the eskies.\
He had a song.
